+# External Video Output on a Blackmagic DeckLink card
+
+FilmCraft can show the Program monitor on a client monitor or TV through a Blackmagic DeckLink
+card, so a grade or a cut can be judged on a calibrated display instead of the computer screen.
+This first version is deliberately small: Linux, one card, one video mode, picture only.
+
+> **Experimental, use at your own risk.** This is an unofficial contribution, tested on one machine
+> with one card (see *Tested hardware*). It comes with no warranty and no support, as stated in
+> FilmCraft's licence (MIT OR Apache-2.0, "AS IS"). The author is not liable for any damage to
+> hardware, software or data, or for any other loss, arising from its use.
+
+## What it does
+
+- Sends the **Program monitor's sequence picture** to **DeckLink device 0** as
+  **1920 x 1080, 25p, 8-bit Y'CbCr 4:2:2 (UYVY), BT.709 limited range**, SDR.
+- **Paused or scrubbing:** the frame at the playhead is shown.
+- **Playing:** the frames are prepared ahead and each one goes to the card as soon as it is ready;
+  the card shows it at its next 25p frame boundary. A frame that is not ready in time is skipped
+  and the card holds the previous one.
+- Sequences that are not 1920 x 1080 are scaled to fit, keeping their aspect ratio, with black
+  bars (larger ones are rendered at the fitting size; smaller ones are enlarged by pixel
+  repetition).
+- The picture is the one the Program monitor shows in Composite mode: effects, colour grading,
+  titles and captions included; the monitor's overlays (safe margins, guides, the alpha or
+  channel display modes, the multi-camera grid, comparison view) are not.
+
+### How it works
+
+| Part | Where |
+|---|---|
+| Card access: a small C ABI over the DeckLink API, compiled with `cc`; `libDeckLinkAPI.so` is loaded at run time with `dlopen` | `crates/platform/decklink/shim.cpp`, `crates/platform/build.rs` |
+| Output thread, R'G'B'A to UYVY conversion (integer BT.709 tables), test bars | `crates/platform/src/decklink.rs` (`unsafe` only in `src/decklink/ffi.rs`, per [ADR 0001](adr/0001-platform-ffi.md)) |
+| Following the Program monitor, frame scheduling, the GPU stage, the setting | `crates/ui-egui/src/transmit.rs`, `crates/ui-egui/src/hooks.rs` (`VideoOutput`) |
+| The desktop app's `VideoOutput` (DeckLink) | `apps/filmcraft/src/video_out.rs` |
+| GPU composite and read-back as sRGB RGBA8 | `filmcraft_gpu::ExportRenderer::composite_rgba8` |
+
+Frames are composited on the GPU (an off-screen device, on a thread of its own) and read back,
+about 5–10 ms a 1080p frame; without a usable GPU the CPU compositor's frames are used instead,
+which is too slow for steady playback of 1080p material (about 60 ms a frame) but fine paused.
+
+## Requirements
+
+- **Linux** (x86_64 tested). On macOS and Windows the setting stays greyed out.
+- A **Blackmagic DeckLink** card that can output 1080p25 8-bit YUV (any current DeckLink or
+  UltraStudio does).
+- **Blackmagic Desktop Video** (the driver) installed and the card's firmware up to date:
+  `BlackmagicFirmwareUpdater status` should say `OK`. Desktop Video is a free download from
+  Blackmagic's support site; its Linux package installs `/usr/lib/libDeckLinkAPI.so`.
+  If the same machine dual-boots Windows, use the **same Desktop Video version on both systems**,
+  or the card asks for a firmware change and a restart every time the system changes.
+- A GPU with a Vulkan driver is strongly recommended for smooth playback.
+
+Building needs a C++ compiler (`g++`, e.g. Ubuntu's `build-essential`) in addition to the usual
+FilmCraft build requirements ([contributing.md](contributing.md)). No Blackmagic software or SDK
+is needed to build: the DeckLink SDK include files are in `crates/platform/decklink/sdk/`.
+
+## Build
+
+```sh
+cargo build --release -p filmcraft -p filmcraft-cli
+```
+
+Build the two together: `filmcraft-cli` built alone has no GPU backend (wgpu's backends come from
+the desktop app's features), so its GPU measurements would report "no off-screen device".
+
+## Use it in the desktop app
+
+1. Connect the display to the card's HDMI or SDI output and set the display to 1080p25 / 50 Hz if
+   it does not follow the signal.
+2. Start FilmCraft: `./target/release/filmcraft`.
+3. **Settings ▸ Playback ▸ Enable External Video Output (Blackmagic DeckLink, 1080p25)**.
+   The card opens at once and shows the paused Program frame.
+4. Play as usual. Turning the setting off releases the card.
+
+Only one program can use the card's output at a time: close Media Express, DaVinci Resolve or a
+running `filmcraft-cli decklink-test` first. If the card is missing or busy, FilmCraft logs one
+warning (`External Video Output is on but unavailable: …`) and carries on; turn the setting off
+and on again to retry.
+
+While playing, the log gets a line every 5 seconds, for example:
+
+```text
+External Video Output (GPU): 25.0 frames/s sent, 0 skipped in 5.0 s; GPU composite + read-back 6.1 ms/frame; card: 1250 shown, 0 refused, UYVY 6.3 ms, push 1.2 ms per frame
+```
+
+### Use a 25p timeline
+
+The card runs at exactly 25 frames per second. For smooth motion:
+
+- Set the **sequence to 25 fps** (Sequence Settings) and use **25p (or 50p) material**.
+- Do not mix frame rates on the timeline: 23.976, 29.97 or 59.94 fps clips in a 25 fps
+  sequence, or a 23.976 / 29.97 sequence on the 25p output, are shown with repeated or dropped
+  frames (judder). This is expected with a fixed 25p output, not a fault of the card.
+
+## Check the card without the GUI
+
+```sh
+# 75 % colour bars for 30 s
+./target/release/filmcraft-cli decklink-test --hold 30
+
+# a project frame (at 12 s) for 30 s
+./target/release/filmcraft-cli --project my.fcproj decklink-test --seconds 12 --hold 30
+./target/release/filmcraft-cli --demo decklink-test --hold 30   # the built-in demo project
+```
+
+`decklink-test` exits with an error if the card is missing, busy or refuses the picture.
+
+## Measure where the time goes
+
+```sh
+./target/release/filmcraft-cli --demo decklink-bench --frames 100
+./target/release/filmcraft-cli --project my.fcproj decklink-bench --seconds 30 --frames 100 [--no-card]
+```
+
+It prints the time per frame of the CPU render, the GPU composite + read-back (decoding
+included), the UYVY conversion and, unless `--no-card`, how the card took 25 fps output.
+Measured on the tested machine (demo project, 1080p):
+
+| Stage | Time per frame |
+|---|---|
+| CPU render to RGBA8 (fallback path) | ~58 ms |
+| GPU plan + composite + read-back, decoding included | ~20 ms (composite + read-back alone ~5–10 ms) |
+| RGBA8 to UYVY | ~6 ms |
+
+The UI tests have a throughput benchmark, run on an idle machine:
+
+```sh
+cargo test --release -p filmcraft-ui-egui --test it transmit_ui::bench -- --ignored --nocapture
+```
+
+## Tests
+
+```sh
+cargo test --release -p filmcraft-platform --lib decklink     # conversion, fitting, bars
+cargo test --release -p filmcraft-ui-egui --lib transmit
+cargo test --release -p filmcraft-ui-egui --test it transmit_ui
+```
+
+The UI tests use a stand-in card; on a machine with a card, the `decklink` unit tests also open
+(and close) the real one.
+
+## Limitations
+
+- Linux only; DeckLink device 0 only; 1080p25 only; 8-bit SDR BT.709 only.
+- **No audio** on the card (sound plays on the computer's audio device as usual). Allow for the
+  display's own latency when judging lip sync.
+- Frames go to the card with `DisplayVideoFrameSync`, not DeckLink scheduled playback: pacing
+  follows FilmCraft's playback clock and screen refresh, so an occasional frame can repeat.
+- The output follows the Program monitor while it is drawn; the Source monitor is not sent.
+- No AJA, Mercury Transmit or other devices; no output of the multi-camera grid.
+- On the CPU fallback path, playback of 1080p material skips frames.
+
+## Tested hardware
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| Card | Blackmagic DeckLink Mini Monitor HD (PCIe), HDMI to a consumer TV |
+| Driver | Blackmagic Desktop Video 16.5 (DeckLink API 16.5; the vendored headers are API 12.0) |
+| System | Linux (x86_64), NVIDIA GPU (proprietary driver, Vulkan) |
+| Result | steady 1080p25 playback of a 25 fps sequence with 25p material |
+
+## Licences
+
+FilmCraft's own code here is MIT OR Apache-2.0. The DeckLink SDK include files in
+`crates/platform/decklink/sdk/` are Blackmagic Design's, under the Boost Software License
+(BSL-1.0) text reproduced at the top of each file, which permits redistribution with that notice;
